course: dictionary
lesson: 14849
title: Rancour: anger that will not fade
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Rancour is a strong word for a strong feeling. It means bitter, deep anger or hatred that lasts a long time after someone has hurt you. You can find clues in the sentence: «Even after twenty years, the two families still felt rancour for each other.» Twenty years is a very long time, so the anger has stayed.
explain: Look for the words around it. «She forgave her brother and let go of her rancour.» Forgiving someone means letting go of rancour, so rancour is the anger she was holding on to. Ask yourself: what would she need to put down? Then test your meaning in the sentence.
explain: The phrase «without rancour» is useful to know. «He lost the race without rancour and shook the winner's hand.» He was not bitter or angry about losing, so without rancour means calmly, with no bitterness.
fact: Rancour means bitter, deep anger or hatred that lasts a long time after someone has hurt you.
fact: «Without rancour» means calmly, without bitterness.
fact: Rancour is a noun you cannot count, so say «some rancour», not «a rancour».
fact: In British English it is spelled rancour; in American English it is spelled rancor.
fact: Stories and history often show rancour between families or countries, and forgiving means letting go of it.
exercise: «Even after twenty years, the two families still felt rancour for each other.» What does rancour mean? | A loud noise in the street | Happy friendship that never ends | A small, quick laugh | *Bitter, deep anger that lasts a long time
exercise: «She forgave her brother and let go of her rancour.» What was she letting go of? | Her school bag | Her favourite coat | *Her bitter anger towards him
exercise: «He lost the race without rancour and shook the winner's hand.» What does without rancour mean? | Quickly, before the start | Angrily, shouting at everyone | *Calmly, without bitterness | Sadly, with tears
test: Which word is closest in meaning to rancour? | Patience | *Bitterness | Laughter | Joy
test: «The old quarrel left rancour between the two countries for a hundred years.» What does «for a hundred years» tell you about the rancour? | It came from heavy rain | It was a short, happy time | *The bitter anger lasted a very long time | It was only a day
exam: «Sam still held rancour against Ella and would not speak to her for a month.» What does rancour mean in this sentence? | Tiredness after running | *Bitter anger that lasts a long time | Happy surprise | Sudden fear
